Intensity of $magnetization
\begin{aligned}
& =\frac{M_{\text{net }}}{\text{ Volume }} \\
& =\frac{M_{\text{net }}}{\text{ length } \times \text{ area$ of $cross-section }} \\
& =\frac{6}{\left(4 \times 10^{-2}\right) \times\left(2 \times 10^{-4}\right)}=7.5 \times 10^5 \mathrm{ A} / \mathrm{m}
\end{aligned}$

Relative permeability, $\mu_{\mathrm{r}}=1+\mathrm{x}
\therefore \mathrm{x}=\mu_{\mathrm{r}}-1=0.85-1=-0.15
$

The time period of oscillations of magnet T=2 $\pi \sqrt{\left(\frac{I}{M H}\right)}$ where $I=$ moment of inertia of magnet $=\frac{mL^{2}}{12}(m$, being the mass of magnet $)M=$ pole strength $\times L$ and $H=$ horizontal component of earth's magnetic field. When the three equal parts of magnet are placed on one another with their like poles together, then $\begin{aligned} I^{\prime} &=\frac{1}{12}\left(\frac{m}{3}\right) \times\left(\frac{L}{3}\right)^{2} \times3 \\
&=\frac{1}{12} \frac{mL^{2}}{9}=\frac{I}{9}
\end{aligned}$ and $\quad M^{\prime}=$ pole strength $\times \frac{L}{3} \times 3=M$
Hence, $T^{\prime}=2 \pi \sqrt{\left(\frac{I / 9}{M H}\right)} \begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow \quadT^{\prime} &=\frac{1}{3} \times T \\
T^{\prime} &=\frac{2}{3} \mathrm{ s}
\end{aligned}$
